    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d221">

                <pb facs="0336" n="328" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg328"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 P. W./264: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-07-18T16:00:00+09:00">July 18, 1941—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 18—6:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1026. For the Acting Secretary. Department’s 404, July 16, 6 p.m.<anchor n="30"
                        xml:id="d221-f1"/> It would seem that the Japanese Ambassador’s
                    interpretation of article 3 of the Tripartite Treaty is substantially that of
                    the Prime Minister (please see Embassy’s 924, July 2nd, 7 p.m.<anchor n="31"
                        xml:id="d221-f2"/>) and not that of Mr. Matsuoka. (Please see Embassy’s 673,
                    May 14, 5 p.m.<anchor n="32" xml:id="d221-f3"/> and 707, May 19, 10 p.m.<anchor
                        n="32a" xml:id="d221-f4"/>)</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
